{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent high bias: forecasts are generally above realized prices by ~0.2–1.5 EUR for much of the path; apply a small downward level shift (≈ -0.5 to -1.0) as a default calibration.",
    "Over-optimistic persistence after peaks: when history has a recent upswing/spike, the model keeps prices elevated too long; increase mean-reversion strength (pull forecasts back toward the 30d mean) over the next 1–3 weeks.",
    "Mid-horizon overshoot: around days ~10–22 the model frequently overshoots (predicts ~69–70 while outcomes drift down toward ~66–68); cap mid-horizon upside by blending more heavily with recent average (e.g., 50–70% weight on 30d mean).",
    "Late-horizon direction errors: in several cases the last 5–10 days are forecast as a decline toward low 60s while outcomes rebound to high 60s/low 70s; reduce late-horizon trend extrapolation and enforce reversion toward the latest spot/short-term mean.",
    "Asymmetric error: the model underestimates rebound risk after a projected selloff; if the forecast path drops >2 EUR below the last observed price, add a rebound adjustment (+1 to +3 EUR) spread over the final third of the horizon."
  ]
}
